<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">QAQ: Bidirectional Semantic Coherence for Selecting High-Quality Synthetic Code Instructions</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jiayin Lei
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Equal contribution. <sup id="id1.1" class="ltx_sup">†</sup>Corresponding author.</span></span></span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Beijing University of Technology, Beijing, 100124, China
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Ming Ma
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Institute of Neuroscience, State Key Laboratory of Brain Cognition and Brain-inspiredIntelligence Technology, Center for Excellence in Brain Science and Intelligence Technology,Chinese Academy of Sciences, Shanghai, 200031, China
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yunxi Duan
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Beijing University of Technology, Beijing, 100124, China
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Chenxi Li
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Chicago, Chicago, IL, USA<span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">xx_sg@emails.bjut.edu.cn, {mam2022, tyang}@ion.ac.cn</span>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Tianming Yang
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author"><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing, 100049, China
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Synthetic data has become essential for training code generation models, yet it introduces significant noise and hallucinations that are difficult to detect with current metrics. Existing data selection methods like Instruction-Following Difficulty (IFD) typically assess how hard a model generates an answer given a query (<math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A|Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A|Q</annotation></semantics></math>). However, this metric is ambiguous on noisy synthetic data, where low probability can distinguish between intrinsic task complexity and model-generated hallucinations. Here, we propose QAQ, a novel data selection framework that evaluates data quality from the reverse direction: how well can the answer predict the query (<math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q|A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q|A</annotation></semantics></math>)? We define Reverse Mutual Information (RMI) to quantify the information gain about the query conditioned on the answer. Our analyses reveal that both extremes of RMI signal quality issues: low RMI indicates semantic misalignment, while excessively high RMI
may contain defect patterns that LLMs easily recognize.
Furthermore, we introduce a selection strategy based on the disagreement between strong and weak models to identify samples that are valid yet challenging. Experiments on the WarriorCoder dataset demonstrate that selecting just 25% of data using stratified RMI achieves comparable performance to full-data training, significantly outperforming existing data selection methods. Our approach highlights the importance of bidirectional semantic coherence in synthetic data curation, offering a scalable pathway to reduce computational costs without sacrificing model capability.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S0.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.12165v1/figures/fig_1.png" id="S0.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:333/625;" width="333" height="625" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span><span id="S0.F1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(a)</span> Two common failure modes in synthetic <math id="S0.F1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Q,A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Q,A)</annotation></semantics></math>: keyword echo/paraphrase (high RMI) and misalignment (low RMI). <span id="S0.F1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(b)</span> The comparisons between our method and
traditional IFD method. Unlike previous work, QAQ scores question predictability given the answer <math id="S0.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Q|A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Q|A)</annotation></semantics></math> and computes RMI.
We keep the mid-range “sweet spot” and filter both extremes.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Synthetic data has become the dominant paradigm for training code generation models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Roziere et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Seedless data synthesis methods like Magpie <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and WarriorCoder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2025</a>)</cite> generate diverse instruction-response pairs by prompting aligned LLMs directly, without relying on human-curated seed data. While this approach enables unprecedented diversity by extracting knowledge directly from model interactions, it also produces massive amounts of noisy data that are difficult to curate.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The quality issues in synthetic data are subtle and often undetectable by surface-level metrics. We observe two failure modes in practice: (1) queries containing fabricated terminology (e.g., “convert a gritty sulla matrix”), where models generate syntactically valid code that echoes these made-up terms, producing high surface-level quality scores while providing little learning signal; (2) off-topic queries that should not appear in a code dataset at all (e.g., a greeting “Hi!”), paired with incoherent responses (e.g., starting with “compensated…”)—indicating a completely broken Q-A pair. These failure modes are difficult for answer-focused metrics to detect.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Notably, methods like WarriorCoder focus on <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">answer quality</span>: multiple models generate competing solutions, and the highest-scored answer is selected for each query. However, this pipeline largely overlooks <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">query quality</span>. A poorly-specified or semantically incoherent query, even when paired with a technically correct answer, provides limited learning signal.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Existing data selection methods like Instruction-Following Difficulty (IFD) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024b</a>)</cite> operate in the <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A|Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A|Q</annotation></semantics></math> direction.
Although mutual information is theoretically symmetric, due to the autoregressive nature of language models, its practical estimation via conditional perplexity is not necessarily consistent. Empirically, we find low correlation (<math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=0.252" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.252</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=0.252</annotation></semantics></math>) between the two, confirming they capture distinct aspects of data quality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We propose <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">QAQ</span> (Query, Answer-Query), a method that measures quality from the reverse direction: <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{PPL}(Q|A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>PPL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{PPL}(Q|A)</annotation></semantics></math>, which captures how well the answer <span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">explains</span> the question. The intuition is analogous to a student who struggles to understand a problem, but finds clarity after seeing the solution. We define RMI as <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log\text{PPL}(Q)-\log\text{PPL}(Q|A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mtext>PPL</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mtext>PPL</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\text{PPL}(Q)-\log\text{PPL}(Q|A)</annotation></semantics></math>, which captures the information gain that the answer provides about the question:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Low RMI</span>: The answer provides little information gain about the question; seeing the answer does not help predict the question.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Very high RMI</span>:
The answer makes the query trivially predictable, often due to superficial or shortcut patterns such as paraphrasing or repetition.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, we observe that strong and weak models disagree on sample quality. Samples where only the strong model recognizes high RMI may contain sophisticated patterns that weak models cannot capture. We call this the “cognitive gap”. Selecting samples with high cognitive gap yields better training signal than selecting samples where both models agree.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Our contributions are:</p>
<ul id="S1.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We identify that seedless synthetic data pipelines overlook query quality, and propose QAQ, a data selection method based on Reverse Mutual Information (RMI) that captures query-answer semantic alignment.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We empirically show that RMI serves as an effective quality indicator, where both misaligned and shortcut-dominated samples can be filtered through its value distribution.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce model disagreement as a selection signal, capturing samples with high “cognitive gap” between strong and weak models.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We demonstrate that 25% of data selected by our method achieves comparable performance to full-data training while outperforming existing data selection methods.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.12165v1/fig_2_3.png" id="S1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/239;" width="429" height="239" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Overall workflow of the QAQ framework for synthetic code data selection.
<span id="S1.F2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(Top-left)</span> RMI is calculated as the information gain provided by the answer about the query, using reverse perplexity.
<span id="S1.F2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(Bottom-left)</span> Our analysis identifies that RMI extremes signal quality issues: low RMI indicates semantic misalignment, while excessively high RMI indicates trivial paraphrasing or echoing.
<span id="S1.F2.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(Center)</span> To eliminate the confounding factor of question complexity, RMI is stratified into 10 bins based on <math id="S1.F2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{PPL}(Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>PPL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{PPL}(Q)</annotation></semantics></math> to compute normalized within-bin ranks <math id="S1.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S1.F2.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(Right)</span> The final selection targets the “Cognitive Gap” (<math id="S1.F2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Diff}=r_{s}-r_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Diff</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Diff}=r_{s}-r_{w}</annotation></semantics></math>) between a strong model <math id="S1.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and a weak model <math id="S1.F2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{w}</annotation></semantics></math>. Samples that are recognized as high-quality only by the strong model are retained, resulting in a high-quality 25% subset <math id="S1.F2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> that provides the strongest learning signal.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Data Selection for Instruction Tuning.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Several approaches have been proposed to select high-quality samples from instruction-tuning datasets. IFD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024b</a>)</cite> measures sample quality using <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{PPL}(A|Q)/\text{PPL}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>PPL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mtext>PPL</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{PPL}(A|Q)/\text{PPL}(A)</annotation></semantics></math>, the ratio of conditional to unconditional answer perplexity. Samples with IFD close to (but less than) 1 are preferred: IFD <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ll 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≪</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ll 1</annotation></semantics></math> suggests the answer is trivially predictable from the instruction, while IFD <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> indicates the instruction actually hinders answer generation. Superfiltering <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024a</a>)</cite> shows that weak models can approximate strong models’ IFD scores, enabling efficient filtering at scale. These methods operate in the <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A|Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A|Q</annotation></semantics></math> direction, measuring generation difficulty rather than query-answer semantic alignment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond perplexity-based methods, gradient-based approaches like LESS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia et al., 2024</a>)</cite> use influence estimation to identify training samples most relevant to target tasks. Multi-dimensional methods like D3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> jointly optimize diversity, difficulty, and dependability via coreset objectives. Recent work explores orthogonal selection criteria: T-SHIRT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu et al., 2025</a>)</cite> improves IFD through token-level selection and neighborhood robustness; SCAR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>)</cite> targets style consistency between instructions and responses; ProDS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>)</cite> aligns selection with task-specific preferences via preference optimization. These methods optimize for diversity, style, or preference alignment, but do not directly assess query-answer semantic coherence.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Synthetic Data for Code Generation.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">High-quality datasets are essential for code generation. Code Alpaca <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chaudhary, 2023</a>)</cite> and WizardCoder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luo et al., 2024</a>)</cite> use Evol-Instruct to evolve seed instructions into more complex programming problems. OSS-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2023</a>)</cite> generates diverse problems by extracting coding concepts from open-source code snippets. These seed-based methods inherit biases from their curated seeds, constraining diversity to the patterns present in the original data. In contrast, seedless approaches like Magpie <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and WarriorCoder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2025</a>)</cite> achieve greater diversity by prompting aligned LLMs directly without relying on human-curated data. However, this freedom comes at a cost: without seed constraints, the generated data contains more noise and semantic inconsistencies. While WarriorCoder selects the highest-scored answer for each query through cross-model evaluation, query quality itself remains largely unaddressed.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Mutual Information in NLP.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Pointwise mutual information (PMI) has been widely used in NLP for measuring word associations and semantic similarity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Church and Hanks, 1990</a>)</cite>. At the token level, PMI helps identify collocations and construct semantic spaces. This concept can be extended to the sequence level, where mutual information between query and answer sequences reflects their semantic coherence.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Model Comparison for Data Quality.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Using multiple models to assess data has proven effective in various contexts. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Helm et al. (2025)</a></cite> compare short-context and long-context models to weight tokens for long-range language modeling. Rho-1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2024</a>)</cite> compares trained and untrained versions of the same model to identify tokens with high “excess loss” for selective pre-training. These works focus on token-level selection, while sample-level selection using model disagreement remains underexplored.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>QAQ: Reverse Mutual Information for Data Selection</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a large-scale instruction-tuning dataset <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{(Q_{i},A_{i})\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{(Q_{i},A_{i})\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> for code generation, our goal is to select a high-quality subset <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}\subset\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\subset\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> that achieves comparable performance to training on the full dataset while significantly reducing computational costs.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Reverse Mutual Information</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prior work such as IFD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>
captures whether the instruction facilitates answer generation. Samples with IFD close to (but less than) 1 are preferred, representing what the model “does not yet know”.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We propose an alternative perspective: instead of measuring generation difficulty, we measure how well the answer <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">explains</span> the question. Specifically, we compute the <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reverse Mutual Information (RMI)</span> as:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{RMI}(Q,A)=\log\text{PPL}(Q)-\log\text{PPL}(Q|A)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>RMI</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mtext>PPL</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mtext>PPL</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{RMI}(Q,A)=\log\text{PPL}(Q)-\log\text{PPL}(Q|A)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{PPL}(Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>PPL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{PPL}(Q)</annotation></semantics></math> is the perplexity of the question alone, and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{PPL}(Q|A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>PPL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{PPL}(Q|A)</annotation></semantics></math> is the perplexity of the question conditioned on the answer. Perplexity is computed as:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\text{PPL}(x)=\exp\left(-\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\log p(x_{i}\mid x_{&lt;i})\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>PPL</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{PPL}(x)=\exp\left(-\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\log p(x_{i}\mid x_{&lt;i})\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of tokens in sequence <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This formulation approximates the mutual information <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(Q;A)=H(Q)-H(Q|A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(Q;A)=H(Q)-H(Q|A)</annotation></semantics></math>, capturing the <span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">information gain</span> that the answer provides about the question. Intuitively:</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">High RMI indicates that seeing the answer makes the question much easier to predict, suggesting strong semantic coherence between Q and A.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Low RMI suggests the answer does not relate well to the question (e.g., copy-paste errors, irrelevant responses).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Comparison with IFD.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While IFD operates in the <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A|Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A|Q</annotation></semantics></math> direction, measuring “instruction-following difficulty”, our approach operates in the <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q|A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q|A</annotation></semantics></math> direction, measuring “answer explanatory power”. We argue that a good code solution should clearly reflect the original problem specification: given the code, one should be able to infer what problem it solves.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Why the Two Directions Differ.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As noted in the introduction, the <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A|Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A|Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q|A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q|A</annotation></semantics></math> directions capture different signals due to length disparity, information density differences, and model capability asymmetry. Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Why the Two Directions Differ. ‣ 3.1 Reverse Mutual Information ‣ 3 QAQ: Reverse Mutual Information for Data Selection ‣ QAQ: Bidirectional Semantic Coherence for Selecting High-Quality Synthetic Code Instructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows the low correlation (<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=0.252" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.252</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=0.252</annotation></semantics></math>) between RMI and IFD, confirming they measure distinct aspects of data quality.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.12165v1/fig_rmi_ifd.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:429/398;" width="429" height="398" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Scatter plot of RMI (Question-based) vs. -log(IFD) (Answer-based). Despite both being mutual information estimates, the Spearman rank correlation is low (<math id="S3.F3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=0.252" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.252</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=0.252</annotation></semantics></math>), suggesting they capture different aspects of data quality.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Implementation of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{PPL}(Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>PPL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{PPL}(Q)</annotation></semantics></math>.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compute <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{PPL}(Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>PPL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{PPL}(Q)</annotation></semantics></math> using the standard chat template, which matches the format used during training:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="86.23" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 653.15 86.23" width="653.15"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,86.23) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#8080FF;" fill="#8080FF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.63 L 0 81.61 C 0 84.16 2.07 86.23 4.63 86.23 L 648.52 86.23 C 651.08 86.23 653.15 84.16 653.15 81.61 L 653.15 4.63 C 653.15 2.07 651.08 0 648.52 0 L 4.63 0 C 2.07 0 0 2.07 0 4.63 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2FF;" fill="#F2F2FF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0.69 4.63 L 0.69 81.61 C 0.69 83.78 2.45 85.54 4.63 85.54 L 648.52 85.54 C 650.7 85.54 652.46 83.78 652.46 81.61 L 652.46 4.63 C 652.46 2.45 650.7 0.69 648.52 0.69 L 4.63 0.69 C 2.45 0.69 0.69 2.45 0.69 4.63 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 20.38 14.42)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:44.26em;--ltx-fo-height:4.29em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="61.23" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 59.31)" width="612.43"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:44.26em;">
<span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">System<span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: Standard system prompt 
<br class="ltx_break"></span>User<span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[Q]</span></span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We compute <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{PPL}(Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>PPL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{PPL}(Q)</annotation></semantics></math> on the question tokens in the user turn via teacher-forcing with per-token length normalization.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Implementation of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{PPL}(Q|A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>PPL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{PPL}(Q|A)</annotation></semantics></math>.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A naive implementation would simply concatenate the answer before the question, but this creates an unnatural input order. Instead, we frame the computation as a <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">reverse generation task</span> using the chat template:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="150.12" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 653.15 150.12" width="653.15"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,150.12) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#8080FF;" fill="#8080FF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.63 L 0 145.49 C 0 148.04 2.07 150.12 4.63 150.12 L 648.52 150.12 C 651.08 150.12 653.15 148.04 653.15 145.49 L 653.15 4.63 C 653.15 2.07 651.08 0 648.52 0 L 4.63 0 C 2.07 0 0 2.07 0 4.63 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2FF;" fill="#F2F2FF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0.69 4.63 L 0.69 145.49 C 0.69 147.66 2.45 149.42 4.63 149.42 L 648.52 149.42 C 650.7 149.42 652.46 147.66 652.46 145.49 L 652.46 4.63 C 652.46 2.45 650.7 0.69 648.52 0.69 L 4.63 0.69 C 2.45 0.69 0.69 2.45 0.69 4.63 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 20.38 14.42)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:44.26em;--ltx-fo-height:8.9em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="125.11" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 123.19)" width="612.43"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:44.26em;">
<span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">System<span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: Standard system prompt 
<br class="ltx_break"></span>User<span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic1.1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TASK: Given an answer, generate the most likely computer science question that this answer is responding to. If the inferred question is outside computer science, respond with ‘‘INVALID’’. Answer: [A]</span> 
<br class="ltx_break"></span>Assistant<span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic1.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic1.1.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[Q]</span></span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We compute <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{PPL}(Q|A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>PPL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{PPL}(Q|A)</annotation></semantics></math> via teacher-forcing on the ground-truth question tokens in the assistant turn, with per-token length normalization. While the two computations use different message roles (user vs. assistant), the RMI score captures how much the answer <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> reduces uncertainty about <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>. The relative difference is meaningful even across different positional contexts.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Validation of Chat Template.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We verify that IFD remains highly robust across template variations (<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=0.957" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.957</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=0.957</annotation></semantics></math>). In contrast, RMI is sensitive to context: removing the template causes a “cold start” in <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="PPL(Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PPL(Q)</annotation></semantics></math> calculation, where the lack of context artificially inflates perplexity and biases the score. This confirms that our template-based implementation is necessary to capture genuine semantic coherence. We provide a detailed sensitivity analysis in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Sensitivity to Chat Template and Task Framing ‣ QAQ: Bidirectional Semantic Coherence for Selecting High-Quality Synthetic Code Instructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Stratified RMI</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Directly filtering by global RMI rankings introduces a confounding factor: question complexity. Simple questions
naturally have low <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{PPL}(Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>PPL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{PPL}(Q)</annotation></semantics></math>, leading to lower RMI scores regardless of answer quality. As shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.2 Stratified RMI ‣ 3 QAQ: Reverse Mutual Information for Data Selection ‣ QAQ: Bidirectional Semantic Coherence for Selecting High-Quality Synthetic Code Instructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, the relationship between RMI and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log\text{PPL}(Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mtext>PPL</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\text{PPL}(Q)</annotation></semantics></math> exhibits clear heteroscedasticity: samples with low <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{PPL}(Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>PPL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{PPL}(Q)</annotation></semantics></math> have narrowly distributed RMI values (concentrated at lower ranges), while samples with high <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{PPL}(Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>PPL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{PPL}(Q)</annotation></semantics></math> show much greater RMI variance. This indicates that RMI and question complexity capture different aspects of data quality. Without stratification, simple but valid questions would be incorrectly filtered out due to their inherently lower RMI ceiling.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.12165v1/fig_rmi_vs_log_ppl.png" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:429/406;" width="429" height="406" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Scatter plot of RMI vs. <math id="S3.F4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log\text{PPL}(Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mtext>PPL</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\text{PPL}(Q)</annotation></semantics></math>. The heteroscedastic pattern (low variance at low PPL, high variance at high PPL) motivates stratification by question complexity.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To address this, we propose <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stratified RMI</span>: we first partition the dataset into <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=10</annotation></semantics></math> bins (deciles) based on <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{PPL}(Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>PPL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{PPL}(Q)</annotation></semantics></math>, then compute RMI rankings independently within each bin. We use <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=10</annotation></semantics></math> as a simple default without extensive tuning, as our goal is to demonstrate the effectiveness of stratification rather than to optimize this hyperparameter. This ensures that samples are compared against others of similar question complexity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Formally, let <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th bin containing samples with similar <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{PPL}(Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>PPL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{PPL}(Q)</annotation></semantics></math> values. For each sample <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Q_{i},A_{i})\in\mathcal{B}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Q_{i},A_{i})\in\mathcal{B}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute its within-bin rank and normalize to <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}^{(k)}=\frac{\text{Rank}(\text{RMI}(Q_{i},A_{i})\mid\mathcal{B}_{k})}{|\mathcal{B}_{k}|}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mtext>Rank</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>RMI</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}^{(k)}=\frac{\text{Rank}(\text{RMI}(Q_{i},A_{i})\mid\mathcal{B}_{k})}{|\mathcal{B}_{k}|}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{B}_{k}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{B}_{k}|</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of samples in bin <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Model Disagreement</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following prior work showing that weak models can approximate strong models’ data quality scores <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>, we first evaluate whether RMI exhibits similar model-invariance. While strong and weak models show high rank correlation (Spearman <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\approx 0.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\approx 0.7</annotation></semantics></math>) and 76.1% overlap in top-50% selection, their downstream performance differs substantially (73.17 vs 68.90 on HumanEval+, Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.2 Main Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ QAQ: Bidirectional Semantic Coherence for Selecting High-Quality Synthetic Code Instructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). This suggests the 24% non-overlapping samples are disproportionately important.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We therefore explore <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">model disagreement</span> as an alternative signal. We compute stratified RMI ranks using two models:</p>
<ul id="S3.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A strong model <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B-Base)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A weak model <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., Qwen3-0.6B)</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For each sample, we obtain two normalized ranks <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{s},r_{w}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{s},r_{w}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> and define:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S3.E4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">Diff</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=r_{s}-r_{w}\quad\text{(disagreement signal)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>w</mi></msub><mtext>(disagreement signal)</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=r_{s}-r_{w}\quad\text{(disagreement signal)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">High Diff indicates samples where the strong model gives high RMI rank while the weak model gives low RMI rank. We hypothesize that such samples are both <span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">valid</span> (recognized by strong model) and <span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">learnable</span> (challenging for weak model). We validate this hypothesis empirically in Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Analysis ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ QAQ: Bidirectional Semantic Coherence for Selecting High-Quality Synthetic Code Instructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>. Our final selection filters samples with <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Diff}&gt;\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Diff</mtext><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Diff}&gt;\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, where we set <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=0.1</annotation></semantics></math> (approximately 25% of samples).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Dataset.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use a reproduction of the WarriorCoder dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2025</a>)</cite> from HuggingFace, containing 329K instruction-response pairs for code generation created through multi-agent collaboration. After filtering samples exceeding 2048 tokens, approximately 310K samples remain. The original dataset is not publicly available.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Models.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For computing RMI scores, we use:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Strong model: DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B-Base <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2024</a>)</cite></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Weak model: Qwen3-0.6B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team, 2025</a>)</cite></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">For fine-tuning evaluation, we train DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B-Base on selected subsets.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training Details.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We fine-tune for 3 epochs using LlamaFactory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Due to the noisy nature of our dataset, we refrain from excessively reducing the batch size. Batch sizes and learning rates are adjusted by dataset size: for full data, we use batch size 512 and learning rate 1.2e-4; for 50% data, batch size 256 and learning rate 0.8e-4; for 25% data, batch size 256 and learning rate 0.4e-4. Additionally, the learning rate scheduler follows a cosine decay with a warmup ratio of 0.2.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate on HumanEval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, HumanEval+, MBPP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Austin et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, and MBPP+ benchmarks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023</a>)</cite> using greedy decoding.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Baselines.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare stratified RMI selection across different percentile ranges and models:</p>
<ul id="S4.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RMI Top 50%: Top 50% by strong model (DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B-Base) stratified RMI</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RMI QW 50%: Top 50% by weak model (Qwen3-0.6B) stratified RMI</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RMI 50-75%: Middle range by strong model stratified RMI</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RMI Top/Bottom-25%: Extreme ranges by strong model stratified RMI</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Random: Randomly sample 25% of the data</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">IFD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>: Top 25% samples with IFD score closest to 1, computed using DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B-Base</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RDS+ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ivison et al., 2025</a>)</cite>: Representation-based selection with task-specific seeds (25 from HumanEval, 25 from MBPP)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i8" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">SCAR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>)</cite>: Top 25% by style consistency score, computed using <span id="S4.I2.i8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">lizhuang144/scar-gte-large</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">All perplexity computations use the same chat template format for fair comparison.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Main Results</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We fine-tune DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B-Base on selected subsets from WarriorCoder (310K samples) and evaluate on HumanEval(+) and MBPP(+) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Results are shown in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.2 Main Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ QAQ: Bidirectional Semantic Coherence for Selecting High-Quality Synthetic Code Instructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S4.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:2.0pt;padding-right:2.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method</span></th>
<th id="S4.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:2.0pt;padding-right:2.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">%</span></th>
