<td id="S4.T2.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">Low</td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">High</td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">Filter</td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">
<span id="S4.T2.2.4.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:85.4pt;">
<span id="S4.T2.2.4.4.1.1" class="ltx_p">Easy for strong model</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T2.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">High</td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">Low</td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T2.2.5.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Retain</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">
<span id="S4.T2.2.5.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:85.4pt;">
<span id="S4.T2.2.5.4.1.1" class="ltx_p">Valid (strong) + learnable (weak)</span>
</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Model agreement/disagreement cases.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This explains why Diff-based selection outperforms both single-model selection and consensus-based selection (Sum-High).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Ablation: Disagreement vs. Consensus.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To validate the effectiveness of our disagreement-based selection, we compare four selection strategies using fixed hyperparameters (batch size 256, learning rate 4e-5, warmup ratio 0.2). We report results from the best checkpoint for each method. Results are shown in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Ablation: Disagreement vs. Consensus. ‣ 4.3 Analysis ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ QAQ: Bidirectional Semantic Coherence for Selecting High-Quality Synthetic Code Instructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S4.T3.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T3.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method</span></th>
<th id="S4.T3.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HumanEval</span></th>
<th id="S4.T3.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HumanEval+</span></th>
<th id="S4.T3.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MBPP</span></th>
<th id="S4.T3.2.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MBPP+</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T3.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">Diff-High</th>
<td id="S4.T3.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T3.2.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">77.44</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T3.2.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">71.95</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">71.43</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">58.73</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T3.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">Sum-High</th>
<td id="S4.T3.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">74.39</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">68.90</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">71.16</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">59.52</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T3.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">Sum-Low</th>
<td id="S4.T3.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">71.34</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">65.85</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">66.14</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">55.56</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T3.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">Diff-Low</th>
<td id="S4.T3.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">71.34</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">67.07</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T3.2.5.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">74.87</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T3.2.5.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">62.43</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Ablation on selection strategies (all 25% data).</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Key observations: (1) <span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Diff <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math> Sum</span>: Diff-High outperforms Sum-High by 3 points on HumanEval+ (71.95 vs 68.90), validating that disagreement signals are more informative than consensus. (2) <span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sum-Low is worst</span>: Samples where both models agree on low RMI yield the poorest results, confirming they should be filtered out. (3) <span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Diff-High vs Diff-Low</span>: Diff-High favors HumanEval while Diff-Low favors MBPP. One possible explanation is that strong models identify complex patterns beneficial for challenging tasks, while weak models recognize simpler patterns beneficial for straightforward tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Stability of RMI Across Training.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To verify that RMI captures intrinsic data properties, we compute RMI using the same model before and after fine-tuning. The Pearson correlation between pre-training and post-training RMI scores is <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=0.9539" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9539</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=0.9539</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating that RMI rankings are highly stable across training. This makes RMI suitable for static one-time selection without requiring iterative re-scoring. However, the moderate correlation between strong and weak model rankings (<math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=0.70" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.70</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=0.70</annotation></semantics></math>) suggests that RMI also reflects model-specific knowledge: stronger models recognize quality patterns that weaker models miss. This dual nature of RMI (stable within a model, yet sensitive to model capability) is precisely what enables the Diff signal to capture “cognitive gap” samples.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Sample Overlap Analysis.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We analyze whether disagreement-based and consensus-based selection identify the same samples. Specifically, we compare two 25% selection strategies: (1) Diff-High: samples with high <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{s}-r_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{s}-r_{w}</annotation></semantics></math>, and (2) Sum-High: samples with high <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{s}+r_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{s}+r_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> (“double-high”). The overlap between these two selected sets is only <span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">13.85%</span>, indicating that they capture fundamentally different sample populations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This low overlap explains the performance gap in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Ablation: Disagreement vs. Consensus. ‣ 4.3 Analysis ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ QAQ: Bidirectional Semantic Coherence for Selecting High-Quality Synthetic Code Instructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>: Diff-High and Sum-High achieve 71.95 vs. 68.90 on HumanEval+. We hypothesize that Sum-High samples include many <span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">paraphrasing</span> cases, where the answer trivially echoes the question. Repetition is a common phenomenon in neural text generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Holtzman et al., 2020</a>)</cite>: models tend to repeat input phrases verbatim. Crucially, such repetition is a <span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">simple</span> pattern that both strong and weak models can easily detect, leading to high RMI scores from both (<math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> high, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> high, hence Sum high but Diff low). By selecting Diff-High samples, we naturally filter out these defect patterns and retain samples with genuine semantic content that only strong models recognize.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Additionally, we compare the overlap between strong and weak model top-50% selections (without using the Diff signal). The overlap is 76.1%, substantially higher than the 13.85% above. Despite this high overlap, the two selections yield different downstream performance (Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.2 Main Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ QAQ: Bidirectional Semantic Coherence for Selecting High-Quality Synthetic Code Instructions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), confirming that the remaining 23.9% non-overlapping samples carry significant training signal. The disagreement signal captures this unique subset that neither model alone would prioritize.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We proposed a novel data selection framework for code generation based on reverse mutual information and model disagreement. Our key insight is that measuring “answer explanatory power” via <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{PPL}(Q|A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>PPL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{PPL}(Q|A)</annotation></semantics></math> provides a different and complementary signal to traditional “generation difficulty” measured by <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{PPL}(A|Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>PPL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{PPL}(A|Q)</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, samples with high cognitive gap, where strong models recognize quality that weak models miss, are particularly valuable for training.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our experiments reveal three key findings: (1) RMI-based selection with stratification consistently outperforms IFD-based selection, validating the importance of reverse direction analysis; (2)
RMI outliers at both ends correlate with undesirable samples;
(3) Model disagreement captures a unique subset of valuable samples (only 13.85% overlap with consensus-based selection) that significantly improves training efficiency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Practically, our method achieves comparable performance to full-data training using only 25% of the data, demonstrating that careful data selection can substantially reduce computational costs without sacrificing model capability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Looking forward, our framework opens several research directions: extending the approach to other domains beyond code generation, exploring alternative model pairs for disagreement signals, and investigating curriculum learning strategies that dynamically adjust selection criteria during training.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Limitations</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our work has several limitations that future research should address:</p>
</div>
<section id="Sx1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Single Dataset.</h4>

<div id="Sx1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our experiments are conducted exclusively on the WarriorCoder dataset for code generation. While WarriorCoder represents state-of-the-art seedless synthetic data generation, it may have specific noise patterns that differ from other datasets. Future work should validate the approach on diverse datasets including other code generation corpora (e.g., Evol-Instruct), general instruction-tuning datasets (e.g., Alpaca, WizardLM), and domain-specific datasets.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Model Selection Sensitivity.</h4>

<div id="Sx1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The effectiveness of the disagreement signal depends on the choice of strong and weak models. We use DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B-Base and Qwen3-0.6B based on their availability and capability gap, but the optimal model pair may vary across domains. A systematic study of how model family, size, and training data affect the disagreement signal is needed.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Hyperparameter Tuning.</h4>

<div id="Sx1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use fixed hyperparameters throughout (<math id="Sx1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=10</annotation></semantics></math> bins, <math id="Sx1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=0.1</annotation></semantics></math> threshold) without extensive tuning. While our experiments demonstrate robustness across different selection ratios, more fine-grained hyperparameter search may further improve performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reproducibility.</h4>

<div id="Sx1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Due to time constraints, we do not report results across multiple random seeds. While the large dataset size (310K samples) provides some stability, future work should include confidence intervals.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1.SS0.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Computational Cost.</h4>

<div id="Sx1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Computing RMI scores requires two forward passes per sample (for <math id="Sx1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{PPL}(Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>PPL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{PPL}(Q)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Sx1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{PPL}(Q|A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>PPL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{PPL}(Q|A)</annotation></semantics></math>), which adds overhead compared to standard training. However, this is a one-time cost that enables significant training data reduction.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
